  <section|Findings and evidence><label|findings-and-evidence>

  Each proof was read line by line and its signs, conventions and quantifiers
  were recomputed. Numbering follows the reading order.

  <subsection|Proofs checked with no defect
  found><label|proofs-checked-with-no-defect-found>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|complex-heat.tex.> Positivity is checked from <verbatim|E_t
    = E_{t/2}^2>, and injectivity by iterating
    <verbatim|\<\|\|\>E_{t/2}x\<\|\|\>\<twosuperior\> = \<langle\>x,E_t
    x\<rangle\>>. The construction <verbatim|E(z)=A^z> and
    <verbatim|V(t)=A^{-it}> uses <verbatim|A=E_1>. Writing
    <verbatim|A=e^{-H}> with <verbatim|H\<geq\>0> gives
    <verbatim|V(t)=e^{itH}>. This fixes the sign used later in
    spectral-measure.tex: <verbatim|F_v(t+is)=\<langle\>v,e^{i(t+is)H}v\<rangle\>>
    is bounded on the upper half-plane. Also checked: the identification of
    <verbatim|E(p/n)> with <verbatim|E_{p/n}> by uniqueness of positive
    roots, and the norm-holomorphy bound <verbatim|sup
    \<lambda\>^\<delta\>\|log \<lambda\>\|\<twosuperior\>>. Lemma
    <verbatim|lem:time-fixed-heat> is correct. The coordinate-convention
    paragraph is consistent: <verbatim|C\<eta\>_firstC^T = diag(-1,-1,-1,1)>.

    <item><strong|analytic-boundary.tex.> Width independence is checked:
    <verbatim|E_u R_q E_u = R_p> with padding <verbatim|B_{p+u}=E_uB_pE_u>.
    The real part <verbatim|y_j-y_i-2p \<gtr\> 2p> is positive. The gap bound
    with <verbatim|p=k/8> gives <verbatim|C=\<big-prod\>C_v 8^{a_v}>. The
    positions in the imaginary-time identification are checked: the first
    insertion lands at <verbatim|(0,p)>, and the word is shifted by
    <verbatim|(-x_1,-y_1+p)>. The Cauchy\URiemann step <verbatim|d/ds
    F(t+i(y+sa)) = i\<partial\>_aF>, together with integration by parts,
    gives <verbatim|D_a=-i\<partial\>_a> with the stated sign. The Taylor
    telescoping identity for <verbatim|P'(s)> was recomputed term by term.
    The remainder bound uses <verbatim|k=s\<leq\>1>, so the seminorm bound is
    uniform on the closed ordered cone.

    <item><strong|fourier-support.tex.> <verbatim|g_s'=-D_yg_s> is checked
    from <verbatim|\<partial\>_{t_i}e^{-iX\<centerdot\>p}=-ip_i^0e^{-iX\<centerdot\>p}>.
    The <verbatim|s>-derivative of <verbatim|\<psi\>_{sy}(g_s)> vanishes. The
    support argument uses <verbatim|y(t)=ty+r> with gaps \<geqslant\> 1, the
    bound at <verbatim|k=1>, and <verbatim|h_t\<rightarrow\>0> in
    <verbatim|\<cal-S\>>. The suffix-sum characterization of <verbatim|H_n>
    is correct. Corollary <verbatim|cor:cone-support> is correct:
    <verbatim|\<Lambda\>^{-T}=\<eta\>\<Lambda\>\<eta\>> is again proper
    orthochronous, and both the boost contradiction and the zero total
    momentum hold.

    <item><strong|label-descent.tex.> The generator transport
    <verbatim|K_{d,a}=Q_d(-iL^0_{d,a})Q_d^{-1}=-iL_{d,a}> is correct. So are
    the ODE-uniqueness argument for boosts commuting with inclusions and the
    generation step. The <verbatim|J> computation at lines 90\U95 is correct
    given the input.

    <item><strong|lorentz-covariance.tex.> The boost Ward identity was
    recomputed. At <verbatim|c=i\<sigma\>> one has
    <verbatim|cz_i=-\<sigma\>Y_i>, so <verbatim|T_{i\<sigma\>}=A_\<sigma\>>.
    In the coordinates <verbatim|(Y,x)> the field is
    <verbatim|A_\<sigma\>=\<sigma\>\<b-cal-R\>_a>. With <verbatim|L_a=iK_a>,
    the integrand is exactly <verbatim|A_\<sigma\>(Ff)>, which is
    divergence-free and compactly supported. <verbatim|D> is a horizontal
    strip containing the real axis. Lemma <verbatim|lem:schwartz-motion>
    gives <verbatim|d/ds P_{B_a(s)}f=-\<b-cal-B\>_af>, so <verbatim|A'(0)=0>.
    The reduction <verbatim|\<Lambda\>\<mapsto\>B\<Lambda\>> fixing
    <verbatim|e_0> is correct.

    <item><strong|full-tube.tex.> The margin lemma
    (<verbatim|p\<centerdot\>y=\<Sigma\>_j q_j\<centerdot\>(y_j-y_{j-1})>,
    <verbatim|c=r/2>) is correct, as are the cutoff support radii
    (<verbatim|\<chi\>=1> on <verbatim|K^{(1)}>, support in
    <verbatim|K^{(3)}>) and the bound <verbatim|-y\<centerdot\>p \<leq\>
    3(\|y\|+c)-c\|p\|>. The holomorphy remainder is checked with
    <verbatim|\|h\|\<less\>c/2>. In <verbatim|prop:tube-identification>,
    <verbatim|d(y)\<in\>C_n^\<circ\>> for strictly increasing <verbatim|y>,
    and <verbatim|W_{d(y)}=\<psi\>_y>. In <verbatim|prop:tube-covariance>,
    recomputing <verbatim|(h\<circ\>A^{-1})^\<wedge\>=P_\<Lambda\>\<#125\>>
    and <verbatim|(Ap)\<centerdot\>(\<Lambda\>z)=p\<centerdot\>z> confirms
    the displayed Fourier form.

    <item><strong|heat-vectors.tex.> The Gram identity was recomputed from
    <verbatim|E(w)^*=E(\\bar w)>. The middle factor is
    <verbatim|E(y_1+z_1-p-q-i(s_1-t_1))>, which matches <verbatim|R> at the
    combined heights with real times not negated, as stated. The holomorphy
    of <verbatim|c\<mapsto\>\<langle\>A(\\bar c),B(c)\<rangle\>> and the
    Euclidean point <verbatim|c=i\<tau\>> are correct. The translation
    identity uses <verbatim|E(-ia^0)=V(a^0)>, consistent with
    complex-heat.tex.

    <item><strong|reconstruction.tex.> Checked: <verbatim|K(ab,c)=K(b,a^*c)>,
    the null-space invariance, the bilinearity argument, continuity in the
    test, closability, the locality and label-retention propositions,
    unitarity and strong continuity of the motion, the isometry
    <verbatim|\<cal-V\>> and the intertwining by norm limits.

    <item><strong|boundary-detection.tex.> Checked: the tensor-test density,
    the involution compatibility <verbatim|(gb)^*=g(b^*)>, the continuity of
    <verbatim|g\<mapsto\>K(b,gc)> by basis expansion together with the affine
    form of <verbatim|lem:schwartz-motion>, the J-commutation computation at
    lines 142\U154, the zero-boundary lemma, the reduction to strictly
    ordered times with permutation symmetry and continuity, and the
    nonzero-field corollary.

    <item><strong|spectral-measure.tex.> In the Paley-type lemma, the contour
    bound gives <verbatim|bs+\<varepsilon\>s\<twosuperior\>=-b\<twosuperior\>/(4\<varepsilon\>)>
    at <verbatim|s=-b/(2\<varepsilon\>)>. The neighbourhood <verbatim|A>
    gives <verbatim|m\<leq\>Ce^{-3b\<twosuperior\>/(16\<varepsilon\>)}>. The
    pushforward <verbatim|\<mu\>_{U(\<Lambda\>)v}=(\<Lambda\>^{-T})_*\<mu\>_v>,
    the measure-uniqueness argument and vacuum uniqueness are correct.

    <item><strong|locality.tex.> The imaginary part of
    <verbatim|B_e(ib)(x+iY)> is <verbatim|Y+\\sin b\\,C_ex+(\\cos b-1)P_eY>,
    and <verbatim|C_ea=(\|\<b-up-a\>\|,a^0e)\<in\>V_+> for spacelike
    <verbatim|a>. The exchanged orientation has the same inward vector. The
    complex extension of the label action is correct. Equality of germs on
    the Euclidean slice follows because the image of the Wick map
    <verbatim|M> is a totally real subspace of full dimension: all complex
    derivatives at <verbatim|q> agree, not only the values. The
    partial-boundary identification, including the change of variables on the
    exchanged tube, and the tied profiles <verbatim|a_j=i> are correct. The
    argument avoids the edge-of-the-wedge theorem and Jost points, and it is
    complete as written.
  </itemize-minus>

  <subsection|F1 (gap in an input; affects the Lorentz chain): the reference
  Lorentz action <verbatim|T_d^0> is not constructed in the
  monograph><label|f1-gap-in-an-input-affects-the-lorentz-chain-the-reference-lorentz-action-t_d0-is-not-constructed-in-the-monograph>

  Input <verbatim|input:calibrated-actions> (label-descent.tex:13\U34)
  requires several things on the <em|reference> spaces <verbatim|E_d^0>:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>a continuous representation <verbatim|T_d^0> of the proper
    orthochronous Lorentz group;

    <item>its boost generator equal to <verbatim|-iL^0_{d,a}>;

    <item>agreement with <verbatim|R_d^0> on spatial rotations;

    <item>an antilinear involution <verbatim|J_d^0> commuting with
    <verbatim|T_d^0>.
  </itemize-minus>

  The section's whole Lorentz chain rests on this input: Proposition
  <verbatim|prop:lorentz-label-descent>, Theorem
  <verbatim|thm:lorentz-covariance>, Corollary <verbatim|cor:cone-support>,
  the full tube and locality.

  <verbatim|physical-angular-actions.tex:24\U28> and <verbatim|:304\U311>
  treat this action as "already fixed" and "attached to that same free
  hierarchy". No file under <verbatim|manuscript/existence/> constructs it,
  states it or cites it. The searches were for <verbatim|T_d^0>,
  <verbatim|boost>, <verbatim|Lorentz> and <verbatim|complexif> over all of
  <verbatim|manuscript/existence/*.tex>. Outside this section, "Lorentz"
  occurs only in physical-angular-actions, trace-spectrum, trace-asymptotic,
  statement (about the generalized free field) and phase-fields. None of them
  constructs the reference action.

  The Lean source does prove it. <verbatim|YM.Maxwell.Free.voyage> and
  <verbatim|YM.Maxwell.Free.launched> (<verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/MAXWELL/Descent.lean:173\U181>)
  construct the action and prove the generator identity, spatial agreement
  and star commutation (<verbatim|Boundary.Spins.Descends>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Fields/Star.lean:24\U35>).
  <verbatim|YM.Maxwell.Free.maxwell_supplies>
  (<verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/MAXWELL/Lit.lean:45\U48>) passes the result as the
  <verbatim|vacuum>/<verbatim|rolls> fields of <verbatim|YM.Org.MaxwellData>.
  The file's docstring says "No Lie-group integration theorem assumed", so
  the formal proof is not just a citation.

  Mathematically this is standard: a finite-dimensional rotation
  representation of the free jet/Wick spaces continues holomorphically and
  restricts to the Lorentz group (Weyl's unitary trick). The sign of the
  generator and the commutation with the reflected conjugate
  <verbatim|J^0=\<theta\>\<kappa\>>, however, depend on the project's
  conventions (<verbatim|i^{\<alpha\>_t}> time factors, contour sign
  "evaluates at <verbatim|-It>"). The section's own computation at
  boundary-detection.tex:142\U154 shows that the sign matters:
  <verbatim|\<kappa\>> alone would anticommute with the boosts.

  <strong|Required change:> add a short subsection near the free reference
  hierarchy. It should construct <verbatim|T_d^0>, or cite a precise source
  for it, and verify the four properties above in the project's conventions,
  linked to <verbatim|YM.Maxwell.Free.voyage>/<verbatim|launched>.

  <subsection|F2 (undischarged named assumption): identification of
  involutions><label|f2-undischarged-named-assumption-identification-of-involutions>

  Corollary <verbatim|cor:word-motion-inputs> (boundary-detection.tex:67\U76)
  explicitly assumes that the word-construction involution (the reflected
  conjugate of Subsection <verbatim|ex-reflection>) is the <verbatim|J> of
  Input <verbatim|input:calibrated-actions>. The final assembly
  (<verbatim|assembly.tex:375\U392>) invokes the descent and covariance
  results but does not mention this identification. The ingredients appear to
  be present:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><verbatim|physical-angular-actions.tex:73\U74> says the comparison
    maps <verbatim|U_{n,d}> commute with conjugation and the signed actions,
    which yields <verbatim|J_dQ_d=Q_dJ_d^0> once the signed actions include
    the time reflection;

    <item><verbatim|J_d^0> commuting with <verbatim|T_d^0> is part of F1.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Required change:> one sentence in the assembly, or after
  <verbatim|cor:word-motion-inputs>, discharging the identification with
  these pointers.

  <subsection|Editorial items (minor)><label|editorial-items-minor>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|E1.> fourier-support.tex:138\U140 says that the Lorentz
    covariance hypothesis "is still to be proved from the infinitesimal
    Euclidean rotation identities". It is proved two subsections later, in
    Theorem <verbatim|thm:lorentz-covariance>, conditional on F1. Replace
    with a forward reference.

    <item><strong|E2.> Vacuum uniqueness is argued twice:
    complex-heat.tex:117\U121 and Theorem <verbatim|thm:field-spectrum>
    (spectral-measure.tex:167\U171). Keep one and refer to it from the other.

    <item><strong|E3.> label-descent.tex:76\U79 cites "the explicit reduction
    of <verbatim|\<Lambda\>e_0> in the proof of Theorem
    <verbatim|thm:lorentz-covariance>", which is a forward reference to a
    later subsection. The reduction is short and could be stated as a lemma
    before both uses.

    <item><strong|E4.> reconstruction.tex:140\U145 says that "the forward
    spectral condition and uniqueness of the invariant vacuum require
    additional facts". These are now proved in spectral-measure.tex, so a
    pointer to Theorem <verbatim|thm:field-spectrum> would help the reader.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Required changes and remaining
  uncertainty><label|required-changes-and-remaining-uncertainty>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>F1 is required. F2 is required (one sentence). E1\UE4 are optional.

    <item><strong|Literature locators not re-verified.> The cited sources
    could not be downloaded from this sandbox: requests to the BHV and
    Williams URLs returned HTTP 403 from the proxy. This review therefore did
    not check Trèves Thm 51.6 / Eq. (51.3), Reed\USimon I Thm VII.2, Williams
    Thm 2.19 or BHV Thm D.3.1 p. 397 against the originals. It relies on the
    #472/#473 record in <verbatim|blueprint/background/literature-472.md>,
    which reports Trèves as "checked in the original text". The <em|uses> of
    these results here are standard and their hypotheses hold in the section:
    a bounded self-adjoint operator, a strongly continuous unitary
    representation of \<bbb-R\>\<#2074\>, and nuclearity of
    <verbatim|\<cal-S\>>.

    <item><strong|Not checked:>

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>the upstream inputs themselves: the all-copy slab bound, slab
      padding and insertion rules, Euclidean rotation/permutation covariance
      of the selected hierarchy, reflection positivity, the null-space
      equality <verbatim|ex-no-new-null>, and <verbatim|ex-limits-unit>;

      <item>Lean correspondence of the section's statements;

      <item>whether <verbatim|R_d^0> is itself defined in the manuscript. F1
      concerns only <verbatim|T_d^0> and <verbatim|J_d^0>.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item>No LaTeX or Lean was built for this report; the commands actually
    run are listed on the PR.
  </itemize-minus>

